# G092 — Bulk Renamer and Organizer

*v2. See `bulk-renamer.md` for prose: preview-then-apply separation,
undo log as reversed ops, collision detection before any mutation,
atomic batch rename that handles circular swaps.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Photo Importer

[vault-photo-importer @inbox @destination
    IN ORDER TO
        preview a sequence rename before touching any files
        let the user confirm, apply atomically
        and save the undo log so the whole batch can be reversed

    @photos <- [fs[ls {path: @inbox}]]
    @dir    <- [brn[directory {names: @photos mapped to name}]]
    @rule   <- [brn[sequence {template: "photo_{n}.jpg"}]]
    @ops    <- [brn[preview {dir: @dir, rule: @rule}]]
        || -> #rename-blocked @resistance:reason

    [ui[show-preview {ops: @ops}]]

    sync @user-events {
        confirm ->
            @undo-log <- [brn[apply {dir: @dir, ops: @ops}]]
            for @op in @ops
                [fs[rename
                     {from: @inbox + "/" + @op:from,
                      to:   @destination + "/" + @op:to}]]
            [vault[save
                    {path: ".vault/last-undo.log",
                     content: [log[to-text {log: @undo-log}]]}]]

        undo ->
            @log <- [log[from-text
                           {text: [vault[read-string
                                           {path: ".vault/last-undo.log"}]]}]]
            [brn[undo {dir: @dir, log: @log}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[brn
    directory       {names}
    replace         {find, replace}                   ;; Rule
    prefix          {prefix}                           ;; Rule
    suffix          {suffix}                           ;; Rule (before ext)
    sequence        {template}                         ;; Rule ({n} 1-based)
    preview         {dir, rule}                        ;; || collision | to-exists
    apply           {dir, ops}                         ;; -> undo-log
    undo            {dir, log}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: preview as total validator `where` collisions exist

[brn[preview {dir: @d, rule: @r}]
    IN ORDER TO
        produce exactly the ops apply would run — including same errors
        so the user's trust in the preview is justified

    @ops <- ops produced by @r against @d:entries

    @ops
        where two ops share the same target name
            -> #collision (two sources map to one destination)
        where op:to exists in @d:entries and is not in @ops:from
            -> #to-exists (would silently overwrite an untouched file)
        otherwise
            -> @ops
]

If preview returns ops, apply is guaranteed to succeed. If preview
errors, the user sees the specific reason and can fix the rule before
anything is touched.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: atomic batch removes all sources `where` before any install

[brn[apply {dir: @d, ops: @ops}]
    IN ORDER TO
        execute all @ops atomically so circular swaps (`a→b, b→a`) work
        and return an undo log that exactly reverses the batch

    phase 1: remove all @ops:from names from @d:entries
             (captures original state before any install)
    phase 2: install all @ops:to names in @d:entries
    emit undo log where each op has from and to swapped
]

The two-phase shape is why `{a → b, b → a}` succeeds: both `a` and
`b` are removed before either is re-added. Same discipline SQL table
swaps use; same discipline any correct batch mutation uses.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @d <- [brn[directory
                {names: ["img_01.png", "img_02.png",
                          "img_03.png", "notes.txt"]}]]

    @rule <- [brn[replace {find: "img_", replace: "photo_"}]]
    @ops  <- [brn[preview {dir: @d, rule: @rule}]]
    ;; -> [{img_01.png → photo_01.png}
    ;;     {img_02.png → photo_02.png}
    ;;     {img_03.png → photo_03.png}]

    @undo-log <- [brn[apply {dir: @d, ops: @ops}]]
    -> [brn[directory-names {dir: @d}]]
    ;; -> [notes.txt, photo_01.png, photo_02.png, photo_03.png]

    [brn[undo {dir: @d, log: @undo-log}]]
    -> [brn[directory-names {dir: @d}]]
    ;; -> [img_01.png, img_02.png, img_03.png, notes.txt]
]
